{
  "disposition": "CHANGES_REQUIRED",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "SYN-PREFACE-001",
      "location": "sections/concise/04-themes.tex:93\u201396",
      "problem": "The summary describes this particular thanksgiving as offered \u2018through Christ.\u2019 In the 1962 Missal, p. 293, no. 1082 (facsimile PDF p. 374), the thanksgiving directly addresses the Father and proceeds to the confession of the Son and the Holy Spirit. Lasance/Walsh, pp. 773\u2013774, follows that wording. The accepted study describes the address and confession in sections/20-commentary.tex:194\u2013201 and sections/30-deliverance-and-waiting.tex:240\u2013247. The proposed interpretation at research/interpretations.md:432\u2013446 uses the phrase. In the concise study it reads as a claim about the Preface's wording.",
      "repair_target": "synthesis",
      "required_result": "Describe the appointed Preface as direct thanksgiving to the Father and retain its confession of distinct persons, one essence and equal majesty. Keep the explicitly interpretive connection between the angels\u2019 praise and the Secret\u2019s prayer for heavenly desire.",
      "severity": "blocking"
    },
    {
      "id": "SYN-REGISTER-001",
      "location": "sections/concise/04-themes.tex:14, 74\u201379, 108\u2013114 and sections/concise/10-commentary.tex:25\u201339, 354\u2013359",
      "problem": "Several explanatory paragraphs end by repeating conclusions the reader has just reached. The themes repeat the exiles\u2019 continuing duties immediately after naming those duties and explaining the delayed return. The Gospel comparison sums up the two readings again after already stating their distinct applications. The themes close with a general statement of continuing need after the two specific statements of that need. The Gradual commentary states remembered rescue twice before drawing out the implications for the Alleluia. The detailed commentary then concludes by restating the two interpretations and the Postcommunion\u2019s petition, all already established in the preceding paragraphs. The repetition reduces the room for close comparison in both authored sections.",
      "repair_target": "synthesis",
      "required_result": "Remove repeated summaries or replace them with the textual step that carries the comparison. For the Gradual and Alleluia, say \u2018In Augustine\u2019s account, the Church can praise a rescue it still awaits. In Bellarmine\u2019s, it can thank God for earlier rescues while asking him for help again.\u2019 At the close of the themes, replace the general statement of need with the change between the orations: \u2018The Collect asks for further remedies. After Communion, the worshippers ask the mysteries they have received to cure the faults that remain.\u2019 Preserve the two interpretations\u2019 distinct consequences and the attributions of their source arguments.",
      "severity": "blocking"
    },
    {
      "id": "SYN-AQUINAS-001",
      "location": "sections/concise/10-commentary.tex:192\u2013200",
      "problem": "The opening sentence calls the ascent of the mind Aquinas\u2019s explanation of Paul\u2019s petition, though Aquinas uses it to distinguish praying from petitioning in Super Colossenses 1, lectio 3, native anchor 87848. The paragraph still conveys both the petitioner\u2019s orientation to God and the reality of the requested gifts. The accepted formulation in sections/40-prayer-and-conversion.tex:85\u201396 states the distinction and its sequence more precisely.",
      "required_result": "State the two acts in order. Aquinas says prayer raises the mind to God and prepares the person to ask. In petition the person asks for particular gifts, which here include knowledge, virtue and endurance.",
      "severity": "advisory"
    }
  ],
